1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,777
Try sorting your classmates by subject.

2
00:00:02,777 --> 00:00:06,332
Some take Biology, some take
Maths, and a few take both.

3
00:00:06,332 --> 00:00:08,960
Where do you write the ones who take both?

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:10,976
Two lists repeat names.

5
00:00:10,976 --> 00:00:12,395
A table gets messy.

6
00:00:12,395 --> 00:00:14,682
Two circles solve it at a glance.

7
00:00:15,132 --> 00:00:17,943
Diya tried this today,
for the class timetable.

8
00:00:18,293 --> 00:00:20,235
Diya and Sana take Biology.

9
00:00:20,235 --> 00:00:22,177
Aarav and Kiran take Maths.

10
00:00:22,177 --> 00:00:23,485
Meera takes both.

11
00:00:23,485 --> 00:00:27,635
And Ravi takes neither, because
he is in the commerce stream.

12
00:00:28,035 --> 00:00:29,678
Where does each name go?

13
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:32,288
A Venn diagram answers it in one picture.

14
00:00:32,938 --> 00:00:35,316
A Venn diagram draws sets as shapes.

15
00:00:35,666 --> 00:00:38,488
The universal set is drawn as a rectangle.

16
00:00:38,488 --> 00:00:41,938
Each of its subsets is
drawn as a circle inside it.

17
00:00:42,338 --> 00:00:46,798
These diagrams are named after the
English logician John Venn, who lived

18
00:00:46,798 --> 00:00:50,071
from eighteen thirty four
to nineteen twenty three.

19
00:00:50,471 --> 00:00:51,712
Now let's sort.

20
00:00:51,712 --> 00:00:53,347
Circle B is Biology.

21
00:00:53,347 --> 00:00:54,873
Circle M is Maths.

22
00:00:54,873 --> 00:00:57,124
Where they overlap means both.

23
00:00:57,524 --> 00:00:59,939
Diya and Sana take only Biology.

24
00:00:59,939 --> 00:01:02,715
They go in the part of B outside M.

25
00:01:03,115 --> 00:01:05,362
Aarav and Kiran take only Maths.

26
00:01:05,362 --> 00:01:07,944
They go in the part of M outside B.

27
00:01:08,344 --> 00:01:11,277
Meera takes both, so
she goes in the overlap.

28
00:01:11,677 --> 00:01:13,013
Ravi takes neither.

29
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:17,455
He is still in the class, so he goes
inside the rectangle, but outside

30
00:01:17,455 --> 00:01:18,442
both circles.

31
00:01:19,092 --> 00:01:23,450
So two overlapping circles cut
the rectangle into four regions.

32
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:25,827
Only B is B minus M.

33
00:01:25,827 --> 00:01:27,804
Only M is M minus B.

34
00:01:28,554 --> 00:01:30,569
Both is B intersection M.

35
00:01:30,569 --> 00:01:33,860
And neither is the
complement of B union M.

36
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:38,863
Every element of U lands in
exactly one of these four regions.

37
00:01:39,513 --> 00:01:40,657
Side by side.

38
00:01:40,657 --> 00:01:43,983
Two circles do not always
overlap the same way.

39
00:01:44,383 --> 00:01:48,921
If every element of A is also
in B, then A is a subset of B.

40
00:01:48,921 --> 00:01:51,459
Its circle sits completely inside B.

41
00:01:51,859 --> 00:01:54,627
If two sets share nothing,
they are disjoint.

42
00:01:54,627 --> 00:01:56,334
Their circles do not touch.

43
00:01:56,734 --> 00:02:00,657
And if they share some elements,
but not all, the circles overlap.

44
00:02:01,257 --> 00:02:02,248
Now you try.

45
00:02:02,248 --> 00:02:06,028
A is the set of squares, and
B is the set of rectangles.

46
00:02:06,028 --> 00:02:08,250
How should their circles be drawn?

47
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:09,481
Pause and decide.

48
00:02:12,681 --> 00:02:13,641
A inside B!

49
00:02:13,641 --> 00:02:15,552
Every square is a rectangle.

50
00:02:15,552 --> 00:02:19,452
So the set of squares is a
subset of the set of rectangles.

51
00:02:20,102 --> 00:02:21,201
Remember this.

52
00:02:21,201 --> 00:02:26,117
One rectangle for U, one circle for
each set, and every element in exactly

53
00:02:26,117 --> 00:02:27,061
one region.

54
00:02:27,761 --> 00:02:32,053
Venn diagrams also show set
operations, as shaded regions.

55
00:02:32,453 --> 00:02:35,200
A union B shades both circles completely.

56
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:38,575
A intersection B shades only the overlap.

57
00:02:38,975 --> 00:02:42,462
A minus B shades A, but
leaves the overlap empty.

58
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:46,646
And A complement shades everything
in the rectangle outside A.

59
00:02:47,296 --> 00:02:50,406
Add a third circle, C, and
the picture gets richer.

60
00:02:50,806 --> 00:02:53,830
Each element now answers three questions.

61
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:55,069
In A or not?

62
00:02:55,069 --> 00:02:56,308
In B or not?

63
00:02:56,308 --> 00:02:57,547
In C or not?

64
00:02:57,947 --> 00:02:58,847
Quick one.

65
00:02:58,845 --> 00:03:03,409
How many regions does the rectangle
have now, counting the part outside

66
00:03:03,409 --> 00:03:04,782
all three circles?

67
00:03:04,782 --> 00:03:06,052
Pause and think.

68
00:03:09,252 --> 00:03:10,152
Eight!

69
00:03:09,863 --> 00:03:13,917
Each element is in A or not,
in B or not, in C or not.

70
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:16,240
Two times two times two is eight.

71
00:03:16,890 --> 00:03:18,625
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:18,975 --> 00:03:21,467
U is the numbers from one to ten.

73
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:23,195
A is the even numbers.

74
00:03:23,195 --> 00:03:25,300
B is the multiples of three.

75
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:28,043
Place every number in the Venn diagram.

76
00:03:28,543 --> 00:03:29,443
Step one.

77
00:03:29,293 --> 00:03:31,837
A is two, four, six, eight and ten.

78
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:33,487
Step two.

79
00:03:33,464 --> 00:03:35,560
B is three, six and nine.

80
00:03:36,310 --> 00:03:37,240
Step three.

81
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,326
Six is in both, so it goes in the overlap.

82
00:03:41,076 --> 00:03:41,976
Step four.

83
00:03:41,925 --> 00:03:44,747
Two, four, eight and ten go in only A.

84
00:03:44,747 --> 00:03:46,769
Three and nine go in only B.

85
00:03:47,519 --> 00:03:48,419
Step five.

86
00:03:48,379 --> 00:03:51,126
One, five and seven are in neither set.

87
00:03:51,126 --> 00:03:54,516
They sit inside the rectangle,
outside both circles.

88
00:03:55,266 --> 00:03:56,166
Count them.

89
00:03:56,162 --> 00:03:58,910
Four, plus one, plus two, plus three.

90
00:03:58,910 --> 00:04:01,813
All ten numbers, each
in exactly one place.

91
00:04:02,463 --> 00:04:03,928
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:08,144
Many students draw only the
circles, and forget the rectangle.

93
00:04:08,544 --> 00:04:11,879
But elements that are in
neither set still belong to U.

94
00:04:11,879 --> 00:04:15,715
Without the rectangle, one, five
and seven have nowhere to go.

95
00:04:16,365 --> 00:04:17,843
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:17,843 --> 00:04:22,804
In JEE Main, as: which Venn diagram
fits a statement, or which set a shaded

97
00:04:22,804 --> 00:04:23,816
region shows.

98
00:04:24,416 --> 00:04:25,625
Exam level now.

99
00:04:25,625 --> 00:04:28,260
Which set does the shaded region show?

100
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:29,522
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:34,922 --> 00:04:36,298
The answer is B.

102
00:04:36,798 --> 00:04:41,530
The shaded parts are the three pieces
where exactly two circles overlap.

103
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:46,184
Option A also covers the centre,
where all three circles overlap.

104
00:04:46,184 --> 00:04:48,194
But the centre is not shaded.

105
00:04:48,694 --> 00:04:53,937
So take all the overlaps of two circles,
and remove the centre, A intersection

106
00:04:53,937 --> 00:04:55,282
B intersection C.

107
00:04:55,282 --> 00:04:56,691
That is option B.

108
00:04:57,291 --> 00:04:58,750
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:00,249
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:03,449 --> 00:05:06,286
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:06,686 --> 00:05:07,586
One.

112
00:05:07,192 --> 00:05:10,613
What does the rectangle in
a Venn diagram stand for?

113
00:05:11,513 --> 00:05:12,413
Two.

114
00:05:11,988 --> 00:05:14,986
How do you draw A, when
A is a subset of B?

115
00:05:15,886 --> 00:05:16,786
Three.

116
00:05:16,545 --> 00:05:18,768
Which region shows A minus B?

117
00:05:19,868 --> 00:05:24,769
Next time: does the order of union and
intersection ever change the answer?

118
00:05:24,769 --> 00:05:27,812
We test it, in properties
of set operations.
